, saxony, germany (1409-) Headquartered in Leipzig. Founded 1409.", "newValue": "Leipzig University, established in 1409 in the city of Leipzig, Saxony, Germany, stands as one of the oldest universities in the world and a beacon of scholarly activity during the Medieval era. Founded amidst the socio-political turbulence of the Holy Roman Empire, it emerged as a center for higher education, attracting scholars and students from across Europe. Key figures in its early history included the university's first rector, Johann von Kriemhild, and notable alumni such as Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz and Johann Wolfgang von Goethe, who would later shape the intellectual landscape of Europe.

